    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Zapier easier to use than Make?</h3>
    <p>Zapier is easier for beginners and simple two- or three-step automations. Its linear Zap builder walks you through trigger and actions step by step, and the interface is clean and unintimidating. Make has a steeper learning curve because of its visual canvas and richer logic options, but once you understand the basics, Make becomes faster to build in for complex workflows with branching, filtering, and error routes.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Zapier or Make?</h3>
    <p>Make is cheaper for most workloads. Make charges per operation, where one operation can include multiple modules in a single scenario, so a 10-module scenario counts as one operation. Zapier charges per task, and each step in a Zap counts as a separate task. For equivalent multi-step workflows, Make often costs 30 to 60 percent less than Zapier at scale. Zapier's entry plans are cheaper, but costs climb quickly as you add steps.</p>

    <h3>Which platform has more app integrations?</h3>
    <p>Zapier has more app integrations, with over 7,000 apps in its directory compared to around 1,800 apps for Make. Zapier's larger library means it is more likely to support niche or less popular tools out of the box. Make still covers all major apps and offers a powerful HTTP module and universal connector that can integrate any app with an API, but for native point-and-click integrations Zapier leads on breadth.</p>

    <h3>Can I migrate from Zapier to Make?</h3>
    <p>Yes, you can migrate from Zapier to Make, but there is no one-click tool. You will need to rebuild each Zap as a Make scenario, mapping triggers and actions to the equivalent modules. Most simple Zaps take 10 to 20 minutes to recreate in Make, while complex multi-step Zaps may take longer because Make's richer logic options can change how you structure the workflow. Make's support team and community offer migration guides for common patterns.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for complex multi-step workflows?</h3>
    <p>Make is better for complex multi-step workflows. Its visual canvas supports branching, parallel paths, iterators, routers, error-handling routes, and variables that persist across the scenario. Zapier supports multi-step Zaps and paths, but logic is more linear and error handling is more limited. For workflows with conditional branches, loops, or per-item processing, Make's visual flow is clearer and more powerful than Zapier's sequential model.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Zapier and Make together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, and many teams do. A common setup is to use Zapier for simple app-to-app connections where breadth of integrations matters, and Make for complex internal workflows where logic and cost matter. You can connect the two through webhooks or via a shared app like Airtable or Google Sheets acting as a queue. Running both gives you Zapier's app library and Make's power without forcing a single-tool compromise.</p>

